  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory remains in effort today for parts \nof the Triangle and south-central NC.\n\nA Heat Advisory remains in effect today for parts of the Triangle \nand south-central NC. Strong heating and high dew points will push \nheat index values to around 103\u2013107\u00b0F across much of the southern \nhalf of central NC and the Triangle, with locally higher values \npossible due to urban heat island effects. Areas of low stratus this \nmorning could limit heating somewhat, introducing some uncertainty, \nbut highs should still reach well into the 90s with dew points \nremaining in the low to mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk of severe \nthunderstorms across the northern half of central NC today. Damaging \nwinds are the primary threat.\n\nA Marginal (Level 1 of 5) risk for severe thunderstorms today across \nnorthern areas. The two main focus areas will include the sea \nbreeze, and along outflows drifting southward into our northern \nzones later today from earlier activity moving across VA.  High \nprecipitable water values near 2 inches and moderate to high \ninstability (SBCAPE 1500\u20133000 J/kg) will support scattered \nthunderstorms, but modest lapse rates and weak deep-layer shear will \nlimit storm organization. Damaging winds from water-loaded \ndowndrafts remain the primary threat through mid-evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Hot Mon, but not as humid, with a chance of \nafternoon and evening storms that warrant mostly a Marginal risk of \ndownburst wind in cntl NC\n\nA strong sub-tropical ridge centered over the lwr MS Valley in \n00Z/16th 500-700 mb data will weaken while retrograding into the srn \nPlains through Mon. It will do so as a lead synoptic trough \nprogresses across ern Canada and a deep cyclone digs along the wrn \nshore of Hudson Bay, and a series of three MCVs evident in regional \nradar data this morning from OH, nrn IL, and wrn KS progress ewd to \nesewd across the Middle Atlantic. The last of those MCVS is forecast \nto progress across the lwr OH Valley and cntl Appalachians Mon, then \nacross the srn Middle Atlantic Mon evening-night. Net, 60-80 meter \nmid-level height falls will have resulted over cntl NC by Mon, after \npeaking last evening (at 00Z/16th). \n\nAt the surface, an Appalachian-lee trough will extend across the srn \nMiddle Atlantic Piedmont on Mon, then probably be overtaken by a \nsurface cold front that will progress across the Middle Atlantic, \nCarolinas, and TN Valley through early Tue.\n\nWhile cntl NC will be solidly in the warm sector on Mon, where \ntemperatures will be well into the 90s, to near 100 F over the \nSandhills, surface dewpoints are likely to mix out into the upr 60s \nto lwr 70s during the afternoon. 36-48 hour back trajectories valid \nMon afternoon originate where mean mixing ratios of 13-16 g/kg were \npresent on 00Z/16th RAOBS at JAN, BMX, and BNA and where surface \ndewpoints were in the mid 60s to lwr 70s F Sat afternoon (versus the \n17-19 g/kg mean mixing ratio values observed along the stationary \nfront and axis of moisture pooling at GSO/MHX). As such, forecast \nheat index values Mon are generally 100-109 F (Advisory criteria for \nroughly all but the nrn Piedmont). In addition to the lower \ndewpoints and humidity values compared to recent days, wly mixed \nlayer wind forecasts in the teens kts will probably yield a wly \nbreeze that will gust to near 20 mph and which may provide some \nrelief from the otherwise hot temperatures. \n\nClusters of storms will probably result immediately downstream of \nthe approaching MCV and across the Appalachians, with initially \nwidely-scattered cells possible along the lee trough. While there is \nsome risk for the upstream clusters to propagate ewd across the \nPiedmont amid 20-30 kts of 700 mb flow, uncertainties surrounding \nboth its coverage and possibly late diurnal timing (mostly during \nthe evening) suggest severe wind risk may ultimately be of the \nMarginal variety in cntl NC.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 4... Heat gradually wanes through the week, with a high \nchance to likely probability of rain/convection Fri-Sat\n\nThe sub-tropical ridge will further retrograde into the Southwest \nthrough the week, while a broad trough will become established \nacross much of the ern US. Related falling heights and increasingly \nmoist, swly flow aloft downstream of the trough will favor both less \nhot temperatures and also a high chance to likely probability of \nrain/convection by Fri-Sat, after a dry mid-week.",
